goroutines: go statements

A goroutine is an OS thread (ADR-007), taken from a pool of reused threads (ADR-026). go
f(a, b) evaluates f and the arguments in the calling goroutine, as defer does, and starts
the call on a new one: gp::go_fn(f, a, b) for a function of the program and
gp::go_call(callable, args...) for anything else. When main returns the program exits,
whatever the other goroutines do, as in Go.

A goroutine's stack is a fixed C++ stack (8 MiB by default), where Go's grows; a very deep
recursion stops sooner than in Go, with Go's "stack overflow" fatal error. Panics in a
goroutine that nothing recovers end the program, as in Go.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#goroutines-and-channels
See also: gocpp explain channels, gocpp explain select, gocpp explain defer
Also known as: goroutine, go, threads

Example (goroutines.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func worker(id int, done chan<- int) { done <- id * id }

    func main() {
    	done := make(chan int)
    	for i := 1; i <= 3; i++ {
    		go worker(i, done)
    	}
    	sum := 0
    	for range 3 {
    		sum += <-done
    	}
    	fmt.Println(sum)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void worker(int64_t id, const gp::chan<int64_t>& done);
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/goroutines.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/goroutines.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:5: func worker(id int, done chan<- int) { done <- id * id }
    // gp[refcount] col 21: done is a const reference: the body never assigns it (a local that no call
    //     can change, or a temporary, is passed as it is: no copy, no count; anything else is copied
    //     first)
    // gp[channels] col 40: send: blocks until a receiver is ready or the buffer has room; sending on a
    //     closed channel panics, on a nil channel blocks forever
    void worker(int64_t id, const gp::chan<int64_t>& done) {
        gp::send(done, id * id);
    }

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:7: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:8: done := make(chan int)
    // gp[channels] col 10: make(chan): allocates the channel; a gp::chan is a shared reference, so
    //     copies and directional views share one channel
        gp::chan<int64_t> done = gp::make_chan<int64_t>();

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:9: for i := 1; i <= 3; i++ {
        for (int64_t i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:10: go worker(i, done)
    // gp[goroutines] col 3: go: the function and its arguments are evaluated now, in this goroutine;
    //     the call runs on a new goroutine (an OS thread, ADR-007/026): a function of the program:
    //     gp::go_fn(f, args...) passes the arguments on to it on the new goroutine
            gp::go_fn(worker, i, done);

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:11: }
        }

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:12: sum := 0
        int64_t sum = 0;

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:13: for range 3 {
    // gp[range] col 2: range over an integer: a counting loop; each iteration has its own copy of the
    //     variable (Go 1.22)
        for (int64_t gp_i0 = 0; gp_i0 < 3; gp_i0++) {

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:14: sum += <-done
    // gp[channels] col 10: receive: blocks until a value arrives; after close it gives the zero value
            sum = sum + gp::recv(done);

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:15: }
        }

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:16: fmt.Println(sum)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
        gp::fmt::Println(sum);

    // ---- ./goroutines.gp:17: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
